In the UK university context, there's the problem that the Unis are
funded by the Government, and most of the 'home' students (i.e. UK
nationals) are having their education paid for by the Government.  To
top that off, there is no 'freedom of speech' clause in the
'constitution', because there is not a 'constitution' in the American
sense of the word.

However, there is a strong tradition in the UK academic community of
resisting any governmental attempts to regulate academic discussion.
So far that seems to do the right thing.  And Bristol, in particular,
gives students a lot freer net access than many UK Unis do - though
there are still some that do better, and I keep pushing us to catch up.

The basic UK student 'right' (insofar as it exists) is the right to
have a computer account for purposes 'necessary for the student's
coursework'.

