Actually, computing has changed amazingly in the last 10 years or so,
and I suspect you'd find that most Universities now take a broader
view of appropriate computer uses for students (and staff).

And, at least at Bristol, the powers you've suggested are far in excess
of what the Computing Service actually has.  Pretty much anything that
could reasonably be construed as 'punishment' must follow specified
University disciplinary procedures, and would require a breach of our
rules.  There are circumstances under which we can, on our own, lock
out individuals, machines, or network segments for investigative or
diagnostic reasons, but we must make these lockouts as short as
possible, and be prepared to justify them.

Our rules essentially fall into 3 categories: those which meet legal
requirements, those which meet contractual obligations to our own
service providers, and those which bar users from maliciously screwing
with other users.  Since most of us don't like being 'enforcers', we
try to keep things simple.

