Michel Simpson (msimpson@hexonx.com) wrote:

> To Jon Noring and al.


> I have gone through the RNewman Web page and 250 K of messages from you and 
> the replies to your messages on a.r.s.

> First, let me say that if I was the running the Church I would not take what 
> is being dished out to them sitting down. And because theirs is a religious 
> business as all religious organization, I would certainly make every effort 
> to reduce the amount of lies being spread to destroy my business. No 
> religious organization with a minimum of balls would let you get away with 
> half the stuff I read. So if they are kicking butt, it does seem like a 
> reasonable reaction to your attacks. 

The church <spit> of Scientology has never made any attempt to reduce
the amount of "lies" being spread by any means that could be regarded
as legitimate or conventional: indeed, it is "tech" not to attempt to
refute lies but to attack the motives of the critic.

This, assuming these alleged lies even exist, is a clear case of
attempting to get two wrongs to make a right, and it doesn't work. If
false statements about the cult are being made here, then all they
need to do is to present sufficient evidence to refute them. Remember
that a lot of the claims being made against the organisation of
Scientology here are being supported with heavyweight legal
documents, such as court transcripts. We're not talking about hearsay
and unsubstantiated allegations here. Yet all that the CoS can muster
in response is a few muttered phrases along the lines of "you cannot
evaluate data", or "I hear that xxxxxxx is a bit late paying his
taxes". Hardly convincing opposition. If the CoS could attack or sue
its critics on a.r.s. into oblivion, you can rest assured that it
would: it is not the weak and puny victim that it likes to
characterise itself as in its endless comparisons with Jews.

> Secondly freedom of religion must imply protection of the faithful from 
> outsiders or the infideles. It would not make sense to say you are free to 
> practice the religion you want but if it not the one we like you will be 
> persecuted and harassed till you are blue in the face. So I suspect 
> legitimate and legal religious organisation must have the right to some 
> protection from people that dissagree with them. 

This all presupposes that Scientology, or at least that part of it
that receives the most criticism here (ie. that part that spends a
third of its budget on legal expenses, most incurred in the course of
BRINGING actions, not defending them) is actually a religion. To me,
that is a debatable point: in my country, Scientology is recognised
neither as a religion nor as a charity, but as a business.

And I am intrigued by this "protection" you feel the cult needs.
Protection from what? Criticism in a forum such as this? Why, early
Christians were being thrown to the lions - doesn't having to accept
informed criticism rather pale into insignificance in that context?
That's what I call *real* persecution!

> Just so you do not confuse where I am coming from I happen to believe that 
> no religious organization is playing with a full deck and in there own way 
> they BS people to get their adhesion and their money. It is a major power 
> game. Catholics the biggest religious organization on this planet(AFAIK) do 
> the same thing but they are much smoother. If that is what catholics want it 
> is their fundamental right at least in North America.

Catholics will not send PIs round to your house in the middle of the
night, or organise demonstrations outside your home. Catholics don't
charge you a small fortune for their version of counselling, nor do
they insist that you take courses at enormous cost. Catholics will
not attempt to drive people to suicide, or sue them into bankruptcy.
In fact, there are no legitimate religions (to my knowledge) that do
such things - Scientology (which I cannot consider to be a legitimate
religion) is the only organisation which does these things.

If Scientology were a "normal" religion or organisation, then why
would it be second only to the US Government in the number of
lawsuits it brings?

> Unless you are aware of something we do not know, the law on the Internet 
> are for all practical purpose non-existant. It is a form of jungle.

> If we built an alt.jon.noring and started shitting all over you I think you 
> would feel justified to wipe out the messages and to request in a legal way 
> that accomplice to this treatment stop permitting this to continue.

The fact is that such a thing is not happening. If a.j.n. were to be
set up, and filled with truthful fact, legitimately gained, then Jon
would not have a leg to stand on as far as preventing it going on. He
would be no more entitled to forge cancels or rmgroup messages than
the CoS is on a.r.s., but he would have recourse to the law if things
being said about him were libellous - ie., untrue. The CoS doesn't
(AFAIK: IANAL) have the right to sue for libel, but it can defend
itself against false allegations. The fact that it hasn't, especially
given its legendary penchant for pursuing legal actions, suggests
that what is being said here is not actionable - and therefore can be
assumed not to be lies.

> If the newsgroup was called alt.shitty.religious.groups a place where all of 
> us could stir the stuff at nauseam... If attacked by one group I would cry 
> foul and be with you 100%.

> But right now you are doing your thing under their name. I doubt any judge 
> in the country would agree with you that they should accept your criticism 
> and just offer you the other cheek. 

Oh, come ON! The newsgroup isn't called alt.religions.the-official-
Scientology-newsgroup: it's called a.r.s. in the same way that comp.
sys.ibm.pc.* has the phrase ibm.pc in it - also a trademark. By your
standard, such groups would be forbidden from criticising IBM for
making grey PC's, or not having co-processors in their 386's.

> Instead of harassing the CoS maybe you just need to set up a newsgroup they 
> cannot attack and then bitch and moan to your heart's content. Everybody is 
> happy. 

a.r.s. was in fact set up by a critic of Scientology. We're not here
to bitch and moan: we're here to expose the excesses of Scientology.
Why should we feel the need to run and hide, leaving Scientology a
forum in which to mouth its platitudes and rehearse its Big Wins? If
they disagree with the points we make, they can debate them. In
Usenet, one voice is as loud as another: barring forged cancels,
no-one can get shouted down. The CoS can defend itself as easily as
others can criticise it. If the criticisms cross the line of
legality, the CoS can have recourse to the law. If the criticisms are
legal but disagreeable to the CoS then it can attempt to refute them:
if it is successful, then those whose minds are open to such
persuasion will no doubt be convinced.

> If your aim is to put them out of business the Net is not the way,the courts 
