es> == erik seielstad <erik@salmon.acs.brockport.edu>

 es> but you need a chain-of-trust.  If someone forges mail from
 es> sethb@panix.com and says - erik, here's my key.  (he may even encrypt
 es> it with my public key)...I add it to my key chain as being from you.
 es> Now whenever "he" posts he can use his key, and I'll think it's valid.

Yes, but if you're trying to maintain a link of identity between "the
author of this earlier post" and "the author of this post", the use of the
same PGP key gives you a reasonable assumption of that link *between those
two posts*.

 es> the "chain-of-trust" doesn't exist.  keeping your key in your .plan
 es> MIGHT be good enough (assuming your account is secure) - but the only
 es> "secure" chain-of-trust that exists, is physically handing your key
 es> to someone.

Incorrect.  If you can, using a tamperproof channel (this includes
"recognizing their voice on the telephone" for most purposes) get the *key
fingerprint* across, the key<->human binding is as secure as MD5.

If you have a chain of safely-signed keys leading to the other person's
key, then that is as secure as the chain; that's what the PGP key trust
parameters are for.

Example: I got a copy of Gene Spafford's key fingerprint at the last
USENIX security seminar.  I then came home and signed his key.  Now, since
he's signed David Vincenzetti's key, I can judge whether or not I trust
spaf's signature to mean it's really David's key.  If I decide it is
David's key, if I trust David enough to sign other keys I can then extend
certification to those, and so on.


